## Track 2: The Clap Was Removed by Wood

**Story & Context:** Eero programs the most direct groove on the EP: crooked kick, bass pulse, and one dry clap that seems likely to become the track’s anchor. Seo-jin assigns a muted geomungo knock to a cancellation rule. Every time she deadens the string immediately after striking it, the next clap is removed from the sequence.

At first the missing clap occurs only once every eight bars. Hae-rin refuses to replace it, leaving a conspicuous pocket where both electronic and acoustic percussion disappear. Min-woo fills that absence with a short bass-sax slap tongue on alternate cycles, so the gap sometimes remains empty and sometimes gains a reed attack without becoming a backbeat.

As the track progresses, Seo-jin uses the mute gesture more often. The programmed groove becomes increasingly skeletal even though Eero never manually removes the clap. During the final section, Hae-rin introduces the sharper janggu head on accents that do not coincide with the missing backbeat, preventing the listener from re-establishing a conventional two-and-four pattern.

The last clap never arrives. One dry geomungo knock replaces it, followed by the mechanical spring-reverb return of an earlier saxophone slap.

