## Track 3: Paper Dries Before the Bass Returns

**Story & Context:** The EP closer begins with the widest low-register sound on the record. Eero sustains a rounded monophonic synth bass while Min-woo plays a breathy bass-sax fifth above it, deliberately overlapping for the first time. The density feels excessive by the band’s standards. Seo-jin responds with one long press-bend on the geomungo, and the established handoff rule begins dismantling the low end.

As the bend continues, Eero shortens the synth notes. Min-woo removes stable pitch and reduces his part to key noise and air. Hae-rin moves from full janggu strokes to fingertip taps on one head. The arrangement dries out section by section, mirroring sheets of hanji losing moisture on the warehouse racks.

For sixteen bars there is no bass instrument at all. Seo-jin plays open strings and small muted figures while a woodblock and faint floor creak keep time. At the expected return, Eero allows only one short synth pulse. Min-woo answers with one low saxophone note, but the two never overlap again.

The final sound is a spring-reverb tail from a geomungo scrape, followed by the natural rustle of hanging paper in the room microphone.

